We will use strategy pattern to solve the problem of duplicate driving capability in both 'OffRoadVehicle' and 'SportVehicle' sub-classes.
NOTE: Whenever the sub-classes is implementing same code (duplicate code) which is not present in the parent class, we will use strategy pattern to solve this problem.

In Strategy pattern, we first create an abstraction of algorithm. This is an interface having the abstract operation. Then we create implementations of this abstraction and these are called strategies.
A client will always call the abstraction, and will pass a context object. This context object will decide which strategy to use.

- We will create a DrivingStrategy interface that will have a method drive().
- We will create two classes: SportDriveStrategy and NormalDriveStrategy that will implement the DrivingStrategy interface.
- We will create a Vehicle class that will have a DrivingStrategy object.
- The Vehicle class will have a method drive() which tells the driving strategy of specific vehicle
- We will create three sub-classes: OffRoadVehicle, SportVehicle and PassengerVehicle, each of which extends the Vehicle class.
- The OffRoadVehicle and SportVehicle classes will pass the SportDriveStrategy to the constructor of the vehicle, which will set the driving strategy in driveObj for the vehicle
- The PassengerVehicle class will pass NormalDriveStrategy to the constructor of the vehicle, which means it will use the default driving strategy of the vehicle.
- Finally, we will create a main class to test the implementation.
- This design will allow us to easily add new driving strategies in the future without modifying the existing code, thus adhering to the Open/Closed Principle (OCP) of SOLID principles.